<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/foundational/metaphysics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Metaphysics asks <strong>what fundamentally exists, and what it is like at the deepest
    level</strong> — the inventory of reality. Not mysticism: rigorous argument about whether
    numbers are real, whether time flows, whether objects persist through change. The answers
    quietly underwrite physics, mathematics and every other discipline's ontology.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four classic questions — existence, universals, time, possibility" widget="equationStep" config={metaCfg} />

  <h2>Identity through change: Theseus's ship</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The puzzle:</strong> replace a ship's planks one by one — is it the same ship? Rebuild the old planks into a second ship — which is the original? Persistence through change is genuinely unresolved.</li>
    <li><strong>The answers on offer:</strong> spatiotemporal continuity (the repaired ship stayed put — it's the same), psychological/biological continuity (for people), or four-dimensionalism (objects are processes in spacetime, "stages" of a 4-D worm).</li>
    <li><strong>Applied everywhere:</strong> personal identity (the philosophy-of-mind lesson), legal personhood (corporations? AIs?), and even biological individuality (colonial organisms, the Ship of Theseus with plankton).</li>
    <li><strong>Why physics intrudes:</strong> relativity's denial of universal simultaneity pushed metaphysicians toward eternalism — physics constrains metaphysics, and the conversation is live.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Loux, Metaphysics: A Contemporary Introduction; Lewis, On the Plurality of Worlds (1986); Lowe, A Survey of Metaphysics.</p>
</TopicPage>
